WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:03.326
A LEFT JOIN is often the most useful outer join in
analytics

00:00:03.353 --> 00:00:09.037
because it lets us preserve a clearly defined
population Here patients

00:00:09.051 --> 00:00:12.948
is on the left That means every patient should
remain in the

00:00:12.974 --> 00:00:19.448
output even if no admission exists Run the query
Look

00:00:19.475 --> 00:00:23.777
for a patient whose admission ID is missing That
NULL does not

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:28.593
mean the join failed It is the expected
representation of an unmatched

00:00:28.619 --> 00:00:34.192
right-side row Now compare this result mentally with
the INNER JOIN from

00:00:34.205 --> 00:00:39.760
the previous slide The ON condition is the same The
difference is

00:00:39.773 --> 00:00:45.431
preservation INNER JOIN removes unmatched patients
LEFT JOIN

00:00:45.444 --> 00:00:51.326
keeps them This distinction matters whenever your
denominator is all customers

00:00:51.523 --> 00:00:56.697
all students all patients or all products If you
accidentally use INNER

00:00:56.723 --> 00:01:00.868
JOIN entities with no activity may disappear and
your analysis can become

00:01:00.895 --> 00:01:06.542
biased You can copy the query into pgAdmin and
verify the same

00:01:06.555 --> 00:01:07.397
behavior there
